---
import StudyHeader from '../../../components/StudyHeader.astro';
import StudyEvidence from '../../../components/StudyEvidence.astro';
import { studies } from '../../../data/studies';
import Site from '../../../layouts/Site.astro';
import RetailStudyBrief from '../../../components/RetailStudyBrief.astro';
import RetailExplorer from '../../../components/RetailExplorer';
import retail from '../../../data/retail-index.json';
import '../../../styles/retail.css';
const primary = ['scenario','mean','seasonal','equal','hindsight'].map(policy=>retail.summaries.find(r => r.factor === 1 && r.priority === 0.8 && r.policy === policy)!);
const compare = retail.comparisons.find(r => r.factor === 1 && r.priority === 0.8 && r.baseline === 'mean');
const fmt = (v: number) => new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', { maximumFractionDigits: 1 }).format(v);
---
<Site title="Which products get the next unit? — retail allocation" description="Explore a constrained retail allocation decision: uncertain invoice activity, shortfall priorities and a shared unit budget. An independent study using UCI Online Retail II.">
  <StudyHeader study={studies.retail} />
  <RetailStudyBrief />
  <section class="tint retail-explorer" id="retail-explorer"><div class="shell">
    <div class="explorer-intro"><h2>Allocate the next week’s allowance</h2><p>Choose a week, a unit budget and how strongly to prioritize shortfalls. The model chooses the allocation. <a href="#retail-model">Review the decision and assumptions ↑</a></p><p>Shortfall and leftover are counted separately for each product. They compare allocation with recorded positive invoice activity; they are not observed lost sales or unsold stock.</p></div>
    <RetailExplorer index={retail} client:load />
    <noscript><p>The default evaluated scenario is displayed. Enable JavaScript to explore other weeks, priorities and product minima, or inspect complete results in the Colab notebook below.</p></noscript>
    <p class="source-credit">Data: Daqing Chen, <a href="https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/502/online+retail+ii">Online Retail II, UCI</a> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.24432/C5CG6D">DOI 10.24432/C5CG6D</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. Filtered UK-customer subset, overlap handling, training-only selection and weekly aggregation. Model: Abdulaziz Aldoseri. No endorsement is implied.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section shell study-prose retail-technical" id="findings">
    <p class="eyebrow">What the comparison supports</p><h2>What the 23 test<br /><span class="serif">weeks show.</span></h2>
    <p>At the reference budget factor of 100% and shortfall priority of 80%, the scenario method has {fmt(Math.abs(compare!.mean_difference))} {compare!.mean_difference < 0 ? 'fewer' : 'more'} weighted mismatch units per week than the eight-week mean rule on average. It has a higher mismatch on {compare!.worse} of the 23 test weeks. The controls above retain every week and expose the other budget and priority settings.</p>
    <p>At this reference setting, the recent-average rule is the stronger observed comparator. The scenario method optimizes its historical scenarios exactly; the held-out result shows why that alone is insufficient when future activity differs.</p>
    <p>The selected history window is <strong>{retail.meta.selected_window} weeks</strong>, chosen on the earlier validation period. These results describe the fixed product cohort, historical activity proxy and stated preference. They do not establish a superior retail operating policy or commercial savings.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Reference setting results"><table><caption>All 23 test weeks · 100% budget · 80% shortfall priority · no protected minimum</caption><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>Mean weighted mismatch</th><th>Mean shortfall</th><th>Mean leftover</th></tr></thead><tbody>{primary.map(r=><tr><th scope="row">{{scenario:'Scenario allocation',mean:'Eight-week mean',seasonal:'Same week last year',equal:'Equal allocation',hindsight:'Perfect-information bound'}[r.policy]}</th><td>{fmt(r.mean_loss)}</td><td>{fmt(r.mean_shortfall)}</td><td>{fmt(r.mean_leftover)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p class="scope-note">The 95% moving-block resampling interval for the mean scenario-minus-rule mismatch is {fmt(compare!.interval[0])} to {fmt(compare!.interval[1])} weighted units. This is a descriptive sensitivity to resampling a short, dependent sequence. It is not a population guarantee or a fresh evaluation on another retailer.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="section tint" id="method"><div class="shell study-prose retail-technical">
    <p class="eyebrow">Evaluation &amp; data quality</p><h2>Keep the comparison traceable.</h2>
    <div class="method-grid"><article><h3>Train, select, then test</h3><p>55 training weeks fix the cohort. The next 26 validation weeks choose one history window from 8, 13 and 26 weeks. All 23 later test weeks are reported. The default week, 4 July 2011, was selected by calendar before evaluation.</p></article><article><h3>Preserve awkward records</h3><p>The overlapping sheets are reconciled once. Primary activity retains exact repeats. Product 84270 stays in the cohort despite no positive test-period activity. Zero-record weeks remain zeros in the activity proxy.</p></article><article><h3>Check alternative targets</h3><p>Separate sensitivities collapse exact repeated lines, remove only uniquely matched same-week reversals, or cap extreme quantities using training-only thresholds. They retain the fixed cohort and selected window.</p></article></div>
    <details class="data-details"><summary>Preparation ledger and sensitivity results</summary><p>Source: 1,067,371 lines in two sheets. Removing the matching 22,523-line overlap leaves 1,044,848. Ordered filters retain UK customer country, positive quantity and price, non-cancellation invoices, a description and a merchandise-code proxy: 935,027 lines across 4,866 codes. At least 26 active training weeks are required; the top 30 eligible products are selected using training quantities, invoice count and code order.</p><p>Reversal matching is a conservative heuristic, using strictly later cancellations with matching product, customer, price and quantity within the same week. Ambiguous pairings are left unmatched. It does not reconstruct physical returns or verified fulfilment.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Alternative activity target results"><table><caption>Reference budget and priority · scenario allocation · each target defined separately</caption><thead><tr><th>Activity target</th><th>Mean weighted mismatch</th><th>Mean shortfall</th><th>Mean leftover</th></tr></thead><tbody>{retail.sensitivities.filter(r=>r.factor===1 && r.priority===0.8 && r.policy==='scenario').map(r=><tr><th scope="row">{{primary_units:'Primary positive invoices',deduplicated_units:'Exact repeats collapsed',same_week_reversal_units:'Unique same-week reversals removed',training_p99_capped_units:'Training-defined extreme cap'}[r.variant]}</th><td>{fmt(r.mean_loss)}</td><td>{fmt(r.mean_shortfall)}</td><td>{fmt(r.mean_leftover)}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><p>Each sensitivity changes the recorded-activity target. Lower mismatch across different targets does not by itself show a better allocation method. Full results for all policies and settings are in the reproduction package.</p></details>
    <p class="scope-note">The data do not reveal on-hand inventory, unfulfilled requests, acquisition costs, lead times or product dimensions. Records are assumed available by week-end; this is a retrospective experiment, not an as-issued operational backtest. The exact model and baselines are verified independently; the website does not control a real retailer’s operations.</p>
  </div></section>
  <StudyEvidence title="Open records. Reproducible decisions." notebook="retail">
<Fragment slot="description"><p>Chen, D. (2012). <em>Online Retail II</em> [Dataset]. UCI Machine Learning Repository. DOI 10.24432/C5CG6D. Retrieved 14 September 2026. The package identifies the source snapshot, transformations, fixed protocol and evaluated results. Only product/week aggregates are included; the original workbook is available from UCI.</p></Fragment>
<p><a href="https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/502/online+retail+ii">Original dataset</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0 licence</a></p>
</StudyEvidence>
</Site>
